{
  "topic_id": "urban_canopy_trees",
  "category": "policy_interventions",
  "title": "Urban Canopy Expansion: Street Trees & Green Corridors",
  "headline": "Dual-Action Cooling and Stormwater Interception",
  "audience_selected": "all",
  "drawer_header": {
    "title": "Urban Canopy Expansion: Street Trees & Green Corridors",
    "badge": "POLICY INTERVENTIONS",
    "summary": "Planting street trees transforms hot asphalt streets into shaded tunnels. Mature tree canopies intercept rainwater before it even hits the ground, while leaf evaporation cools neighborhood air like natural air conditioning."
  },
  "drawer_body_markdown": "## Urban Canopy Expansion: Street Trees & Green Corridors\n*Dual-Action Cooling and Stormwater Interception*\n\n### Accessible Overview\nPlanting street trees transforms hot asphalt streets into shaded tunnels. Mature tree canopies intercept rainwater before it even hits the ground, while leaf evaporation cools neighborhood air like natural air conditioning.\n\n---\n### Urban Canopy Expansion\n\n> **Key Takeaway:** Street trees are the most complete natural infrastructure: they provide shade, cool the air through water vapor, and drink up stormwater.\n\n#### Model Parameters:\n- **Evapotranspiration Index:** $ET_r = 0.90$\n- **Shade Factor:** $0.95$\n- **WorldCover Transition:** Built-up (Class 50) $\\rightarrow$ Tree cover (Class 10)\n\n*Source: Stanford InVEST & UNCCD Trends.Earth.*\n\n#### Mathematical Equations\n- `Net Evapotranspiration ET_r = 0.90`\n- `Canopy Shade Coefficient = 0.95`\n- `Composite Curve Number shift: Class 50 (98) -> Class 10 (60)`\n\n---\n### Biophysical Mechanism & Baseline Assumptions\nFoliage blocks shortwave solar radiation and converts sensible heat to latent heat via transpiration. Roots aerate soil, dramatically increasing infiltration rates.\n\n**Assumptions:**\n- Trees planted are climate-resilient native species.\n- Maintenance programs ensure canopy health and root volume expansion.\n\n---\n### UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDG 15.3.1) Alignment\n- **Indicator**: Indicator 15.3.1: Proportion of land that is degraded over total land area\n- **Custodian**: United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification (UNCCD) & UNSD\n- **Connection**: Restores vegetative productivity (LPD) and transitions land from built-up to tree cover.\n- **Trends.Earth Compliance**: Reverses artificial land degradation under UNCCD Trends.Earth land cover transition guidelines.\n\n---\n### Verified Scientific Citations\n- **InVEST 3.14.0 Urban Cooling Model User Guide & Biophysical Theoretical Background** (2020) - *Stanford University: Natural Capital Project* by Sharp, R., Douglass, J., Wolny, S. et al.. [Source Link](https://invest-userguide.readthedocs.io/en/latest/urban_cooling_model.html)\n- **InVEST Urban Flood Risk Mitigation Model & USDA-SCS Runoff Curve Number Estimation** (2021) - *Stanford University / National Engineering Handbook Section 4* by Stanford Natural Capital Project, USDA Natural Resources Conservation Service (NRCS). [Source Link](https://invest-userguide.readthedocs.io/en/latest/urban_flood_mitigation.html)\n- **SDG Indicator Metadata: Target 15.3, Indicator 15.3.1 (Proportion of land that is degraded over total land area)** (2021) - *United Nations SDG Global Database* by United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification (UNCCD), United Nations Statistics Division (UNSD). [Source Link](https://unstats.un.org/sdgs/metadata/files/Metadata-15-03-01.pdf)",
  "scientific_equations": [
    "Net Evapotranspiration ET_r = 0.90",
    "Canopy Shade Coefficient = 0.95",
    "Composite Curve Number shift: Class 50 (98) -> Class 10 (60)"
  ],
  "key_coefficients": {
    "Unit Cost": "$25,000 per 1% neighborhood conversion",
    "Cooling Impact": "High (Shade + Evapotranspiration)",
    "Runoff Impact": "Moderate (Canopy interception + root infiltration)"
  },
  "un_sdg_framework": {
    "indicator_code": "Indicator 15.3.1: Proportion of land that is degraded over total land area",
    "target_summary": "Target 15.3: By 2030, combat desertification, restore degraded land and soil, including land affected by desertification, drought and floods, and strive to achieve a land degradation-neutral world.",
    "sub_indicators": [
      {
        "name": "Land Cover Transitions (ESA WorldCover / IPCC Categories)",
        "rule": "Transitions from Built-up surfaces (Class 50) back to Vegetated/Tree Canopy (Class 10) or Grassland/Soil (Class 20/30) are evaluated as positive land restoration and avoided artificial land degradation under UNCCD matrix guidelines.",
        "game_connection": "In TurnGreen, de-paving asphalt plazas or planting street tree corridors actively transitions grid cells out of Class 50, reversing localized urban land degradation."
      },
      {
        "name": "Biomass and Net Primary Productivity (NDVI / Phenology)",
        "rule": "Replacing non-evaporative, zero-photosynthesis impervious asphalt with active living biomass (street trees, green roofs, pocket parks) shifts local productivity trajectory from 'Declining/Degraded' to 'Stable' or 'Increasing'.",
        "game_connection": "Tree canopy expansion and bioswale installation introduce active photosynthesis and evapotranspiration into urban micro-corridors, registering productive vegetative growth."
      },
      {
        "name": "Soil Organic Carbon Stocks and Permeability",
        "rule": "Unsealing urban soils through permeable pavement, bioswales, and soil aeration halts soil asphyxiation and re-establishes carbon cycling and organic sponge capacity.",
        "game_connection": "Bioswales and permeable plazas re-open sealed ground, allowing infiltration and soil restoration recognized under international land neutrality criteria."
      }
    ]
  },
  "citations": [
    {
      "title": "InVEST 3.14.0 Urban Cooling Model User Guide & Biophysical Theoretical Background",
